+---
+node: record-store
+---
+
+# Decision: Concurrent Visit Edit Strategy
+
+## Context
+
+A seasonal fieldwork program produces 6,000 visit records per season across 12 shared tablets and 24 workers. Tablets offline for up to 48 hours are routine, and workers independently correct observations after comparing field notes — the same visit can receive edits from different people derived from different starting states. The record store must resolve these concurrent edits into a defensible current observation.
+
+The HLD commits that "Do not silently discard a submitted edit" — no approach can throw away original submissions. Both options under consideration preserve all submissions and differ only in how they become a current record.
+
+The team operates two coordinators without a dedicated systems administrator. The science partner receives a reviewed CSV weekly. The HLD also commits "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable" — every exported observation must be traceable to submitted edits and coordinator decisions.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+**Gates (inherited from HLD):**
+- Must preserve all original submissions with author and server receipt time.
+- Must support independent corrections to the same visit from different workers.
+
+Both options satisfy these.
+
+**Weighted Criteria:**
+
+1. **Coordinator workload and control** (major) — The two coordinators must actively review and approve the exported observations. A large volume of unresolved work creates bottlenecks; automatic merging can reduce it. But automatic merging risks unknowingly combining edits that are scientifically related even if they affect different fields. The coordinators explicitly prefer to see what they're resolving and make conscious choices, even if that means a larger review queue.
+
+2. **Preservation of whole-visit intent** (major, connects to HLD tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit") — A worker who revises a species ID and its cover estimate in one submission is expressing a scientific relationship between those fields. Splitting the changes across a field-by-field merge, or automatically combining with another submission's species entries, can break that intent. The field-aware approach requires maintained rules for what can merge safely (e.g., "observer name corrections can merge with species additions"); those rules are inferred from field names and types, not scientific relationships the server cannot see.
+
+3. **Implementation and maintenance burden** (moderate) — Whole-visit revisions require revision tracking and state machines but no merge logic. Field-aware merge requires and maintaining merge rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species cover data — all of which have different semantics. The rules must be applied consistently across all submissions and revisited if new field types are added. Both approaches require tests; merge logic has more subtle failure modes.
+
+4. **Downstream complexity and consistency** (moderate) — Tablet-sync must provide workers with information about unresolved work and base-revision tracking. Review-export must present coordinators with review work and mark eligible visits for export. Whole-visit revisions: coordinators see competing complete submissions and choose one or manually merge all fields together. Field-aware merge: coordinators see field-level conflicts and apply merge rules, with more complex provenance tracking. The cascade into both downstream nodes is larger for field-aware.
+
+5. **Explainability to science partner** (minor) — The partner consumes a CSV of current observations. Whole-visit revisions: each observation is a submitted or coordinator-resolved version, traceable to a submission. Field-aware merge: some fields may be automatically combined from multiple submissions — harder to explain which edits contributed to which fields.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Whole-Visit Revisions
+
+Each submission is a complete, independent revision of the visit, tagged with the revision it was derived from. When two submissions reference different starting states, they compete. A coordinator chooses one competing revision as current, or manually authors a new merged revision by providing complete new visit data.
+
+**How it works:**
+- Worker submits complete visit data (observation date, all observers, all species, notes), naming the base revision they derived it from.
+- If the base revision is still current, the new submission becomes current immediately.
+- If the base revision is older than the current revision, the visit moves to Unresolved state with two competing revisions awaiting review.
+- Coordinator sees both complete submissions side-by-side and either selects one as current or manually types a merged version.
+- All original submissions are preserved; the coordinator's choice is recorded with a timestamp and rationale.
+
+**Analysis against criteria:**
+
+- **Coordinator workload:** weak fit. Every concurrent edit creates review work, even if the edits are to unrelated fields (e.g., one worker corrects an observer name, another adds a missing species). A coordinator must review both, choose one, or merge them. The team expects to see more unresolved work.
+- **Preservation of whole-visit intent:** strong fit. The worker's complete submission—including the relationship between fields—stays intact. A coordinator's manual merge consciously decides whether to keep both species entries, reorder observers, etc.; no automatic rules hide those decisions.
+- **Implementation burden:** strong fit. Revision state machine is straightforward: Current → Unresolved → Current via resolution. No merge rules to maintain. Submission storage is complete records; no partial updates.
+- **Downstream complexity:** strong fit. Tablet-sync retains base revision IDs and polls for resolution. Review-export shows two complete visits side-by-side with a revision comparison interface. Both are simpler than tracking field-level merges and provenance.
+- **Explainability:** strong fit. Each exported observation is a submitted or coordinator-resolved revision, transparently linked to the submission(s) it came from.
+
+**Summary:** Preserves submitted intent at the cost of larger coordinator queue.
+
+### Field-Aware Three-Way Merge
+
+Each submission provides its base state and the fields it changed. The server combines disjoint changes automatically (e.g., one worker corrects an observer name, another adds a species; both can be kept). Overlapping changes require coordinator review.
+
+**How it works:**
+- Worker submits a base snapshot and a changeset (e.g., "add observer Bob, update Zostera cover from 10% to 15%").
+- Server performs a three-way merge: base state + all changesets received = candidate merged visit.
+- Non-overlapping changes combine automatically (observer edits don't conflict with species edits unless they collide on the species list).
+- Overlapping changes (two submissions both modified the same species entry, or conflicting observer name corrections) stay unresolved.
+- Coordinator reviews overlapping changes and either selects one changeset or manually provides a resolution.
+- Merged visits (auto-combined + resolved overlaps) can enter export without coordinator review; only the overlaps need decisions.
+
+**Analysis against criteria:**
+
+- **Coordinator workload:** strong fit. Disjoint edits combine automatically, reducing review volume. Coordinators only see actual conflicts (two edits to the same field).
+- **Preservation of whole-visit intent:** weak fit. The server's merge rules decide whether "observer name correction" and "species addition" can coexist. Those rules don't know that a worker revised both a species ID and its cover in one submission *because they are related*. If another submission added a different species, the auto-merge rule for "species entries can combine" applies: both species lists merge without flagging the relationship. Coordinators see species overlaps as a conflict only if the same species entry was edited twice.
+- **Implementation burden:** weak fit. Merge rules must be authored, tested, and maintained for every field type. Species lists have complex semantics (are duplicate entries allowed? can entries be reordered?). Observer lists have edge cases (what if both submissions add and remove the same person?). Adding a new field type (e.g., photos) requires new merge rules. Rules are inferred from field names; scientific relationships are not visible, so rules can become outdated if the science team's intent changes.
+- **Downstream complexity:** partial fit. Tablet-sync must send structured changesets instead of complete visits, and retain base snapshots. Review-export must track field-level provenance (which submission contributed which field) and show both auto-merged results and conflicting changes, with rules for which edits can merge. More complex state model.
+- **Explainability:** partial fit. Exported observations are a mix of auto-merged fields (need to explain which submissions contributed to which fields) and coordinator-selected or manually-authored overlaps. Traceability is finer-grained but harder to explain.
+
+**Summary:** Reduces coordinator workload by automatically merging unrelated field changes, at the cost of maintaining merge rules and risking hidden loss of intent.
+
+## Selection
+
+**Whole-visit revisions** is chosen.
+
+This decision turns on two major criteria, both weighting strongly in favor:
+
+1. **Preservation of whole-visit intent** (major criterion, rooted in HLD tenet "Do not silently discard a submitted edit"): The field-aware approach's automatic merge relies on inference from field types, not domain knowledge of why a worker edited multiple fields together. A coordinator sees what was changed but not why. Whole-visit revisions preserve the worker's complete decision in each submission; a coordinator's choice is explicit and traceable.
+
+2. **Coordinator control and explainability** (major criterion, rooted in team preference and HLD tenet "Keep the path from a submitted observation to an exported value inspectable"): The team explicitly prefers visibility and manual decisions over automatic combination. They trust their own judgment more than field-type-based merge rules. Every exported observation should be traceable to the submission(s) and coordinator choice that produced it, without hidden automatic combination rules.
+
+The field-aware approach wins on workload reduction, which is real: a larger coordinator queue is the tradeoff. The team has opted to accept that tradeoff. Two coordinators reviewing a larger set of concurrent edits is preferable to running an automatic merge process that might silently lose scientific intent.
+
+**Downstream obligations:**
+
+- **Tablet-sync** must:
+  - Store base revision IDs with local drafts so workers can see whether the server's current state has moved since their draft was created.
+  - Alert workers to potential conflicts before submission ("the observation you're editing has been updated since your draft; you may be creating competing revisions").
+  - Display unresolved work indicators so workers know their recent submissions are awaiting review.
+  - Refresh local state after resolution so the worker sees the coordinator's decision.
+
+- **Review-export** must:
+  - Exclude unresolved visits from export (only export visits in Current state).
+  - Provide coordinators a revision comparison view (side-by-side diff of competing submissions) so they can make an informed choice.
+  - Track and surface which resolution was applied to each visit (which competing revision was chosen, or whether a manual merge was performed).
+  - Maintain an audit trail so the science partner and internal team can see which coordinator resolved each visit, when, and why.
+
+Both downstream nodes are simpler than they would be under field-aware merge, but they own the responsibility to make that choice visible and traceable to the user.
