-- [ ] **REC-001**: When an authenticated worker submits a valid visit edit, the system SHALL retain the submitted payload with its visit ID, author ID, and server receipt time.
-- [ ] **REC-002**: When a submission repeats an accepted idempotency key with an identical payload, the system SHALL return the original acceptance result without creating another stored submission.
-- [ ] **REC-003**: When a submission repeats an accepted idempotency key with a different payload, the system SHALL reject the submission and leave the original submission unchanged.
-- [ ] **REC-004**: When edits derived from the same starting visit state are accepted, the system SHALL preserve both original submissions regardless of the concurrency policy's outcome.
-- [ ] **REC-005**: When a caller retrieves a visit, the system SHALL indicate whether unresolved review work exists for that visit.
-- [ ] **REC-006**: When a coordinator resolves review work, the system SHALL retain the coordinator's identity and the contributing submission IDs with the resolution outcome.
+## Submission Acceptance and Idempotency
+
+- [ ] **REC-001**: When an authenticated worker submits a valid visit edit with a plot reference, observation date, observers, species data, and optional notes, the system SHALL assign a server receipt timestamp and a unique revision ID, then store the complete submission payload with the visit UUID, base revision ID, author ID, and idempotency key.
+- [ ] **REC-002**: When a submission repeats an accepted idempotency key with identical visit UUID, base revision, and payload, the system SHALL return the original acceptance result (including the original revision ID and receipt time) without creating a new submission.
+- [ ] **REC-003**: When a submission repeats an accepted idempotency key with a different payload or base revision, the system SHALL reject the submission with a conflict error and leave the original submission and visit state unchanged.
+
+## Visit State Transitions
+
+- [ ] **REC-004**: When the first submission for a visit UUID is accepted, the system SHALL set the visit state to **Current** with that submission's revision ID as the current revision.
+- [ ] **REC-005**: When a subsequent submission references the current revision as its base, the system SHALL replace the current revision with the new submission's revision ID and set the visit state to **Current**.
+- [ ] **REC-006**: When a submission references a revision older than the current revision, the system SHALL create a competing revision and set the visit state to **Unresolved**.
+- [ ] **REC-007**: When a visit enters **Unresolved** state, the system SHALL retain all competing submissions and their metadata (submission time, author, base revision).
+
+## Retrieval
+
+- [ ] **REC-008**: When retrieving a visit in **Current** state, the system SHALL return the visit UUID, current revision ID, complete visit data, and the state indicator "current".
+- [ ] **REC-009**: When retrieving a visit in **Unresolved** state, the system SHALL return the visit UUID, complete data for each competing revision (grouped by submission), a count of competing revisions, and the state indicator "unresolved" along with metadata for each competitor (author, base revision ID, submission timestamp).
+- [ ] **REC-010**: When retrieving a visit, the system SHALL not modify the visit state or create audit entries; retrieval is read-only.
+
+## Coordinator Resolution
+
+- [ ] **REC-011**: When a coordinator selects a competing revision as the resolution for an **Unresolved** visit, the system SHALL record the coordinator ID, resolution timestamp, the ID of the winning submission, all submissions being resolved, set the visit state to **Current** with the winning revision as current, and assign the selected revision as the new authoritative revision.
+- [ ] **REC-012**: When a coordinator manually merges competing revisions by providing new visit data, the system SHALL create a new revision ID, author it to the coordinator (not a worker), record the resolution with the coordinator ID, resolution timestamp, all contributing submission IDs, set the visit state to **Current** with the new merged revision, and mark this resolution as "manual merge".
+- [ ] **REC-013**: All original submissions in a resolved visit SHALL remain permanently accessible; resolution does not delete or archive competing revisions.
+
+## Preservation and Audit Trail
+
+- [ ] **REC-014**: The system SHALL retain all submitted payloads, their author IDs, base revision IDs, server receipt times, and idempotency keys indefinitely.
+- [ ] **REC-015**: For each visit resolution, the system SHALL record the coordinator ID, resolution timestamp, resolution type (selected revision vs. manual merge), the IDs of all submissions involved, and the resulting current revision ID.
+- [ ] **REC-016**: When a coordinator reviews the submission history for a visit, the system SHALL return all submissions in chronological order with their metadata (submission time, author, base revision, revision ID), enabling full traceability of how the observation evolved.
+
+## Error Handling and Edge Cases
+
+- [ ] **REC-017**: If a submission references a base revision ID that does not exist (has been deleted or never existed), the system SHALL reject the submission with a "base revision not found" error and return the current revision ID so the submitter can re-fetch the current state.
+- [ ] **REC-018**: If a submission arrives for a visit already in **Unresolved** state and references a base revision older than any existing submission's base, the system SHALL add it to the competing revisions and keep the visit in **Unresolved** state.
+- [ ] **REC-019**: If a submission's plot reference does not exist in the site catalog, the system SHALL reject the submission with a validation error.
+- [ ] **REC-020**: If a submission is missing required fields (observation date, at least one observer, at least one species entry) or contains invalid field types, the system SHALL reject the submission before assigning a revision ID or receipt timestamp.
